-- Upgrade existing Pharmacy Degree sample notes into usable study material.
update public.notes
set content = '<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Distinguish pharmacodynamics from pharmacokinetics.</li><li>Explain dose-response relationships and therapeutic index.</li><li>Connect drug action with intended and adverse effects.</li></ul><h2>What pharmacology studies</h2><p>Pharmacology examines how medicines interact with living systems. It includes pharmacodynamics—the effects a medicine produces and how it produces them—and pharmacokinetics, which describes absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion (ADME). These processes work together to determine the concentration of medicine reaching a target and the response that follows.</p><h2>Core pharmacodynamic concepts</h2><p>A drug target may be a receptor, enzyme, ion channel, transporter or other biological structure. An agonist activates a receptor; an antagonist binds and reduces activation by an agonist. Potency describes the amount needed to produce a specified effect, whereas efficacy describes the maximum effect a drug can produce in a defined system. A more potent drug is not automatically safer or more effective for every patient.</p><h2>Dose and response</h2><p>As dose or concentration increases, response commonly rises until a maximum is approached. The dose-response relationship helps compare medicines, estimate useful doses and recognise when further dose increases are unlikely to improve benefit but may increase harm. Actual response varies with age, organ function, genetics, concurrent medicines and adherence.</p><h2>Therapeutic index and safety</h2><p>The therapeutic index compares toxic and effective dose measures in a population or experimental setting. A narrow therapeutic index means the effective and harmful ranges are close, so careful monitoring and individualisation may be important. The index is a useful concept, not a guarantee that a medicine is safe for a particular person.</p><h2>Apply the concept</h2><p>If two medicines produce the same maximum response but one reaches that response at a lower concentration, the first is more potent in that experiment. Do not conclude it is more efficacious or clinically preferable without considering safety, evidence and patient factors.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Pharmacodynamics asks what a medicine does to the body.</li><li>Pharmacokinetics asks what the body does to a medicine.</li><li>Potency, efficacy and safety are related but distinct concepts.</li><li>Clinical decisions require evidence and patient-specific assessment.</li></ul>',
    content_preview = 'A structured introduction to pharmacology, pharmacodynamics, pharmacokinetics, dose-response relationships and therapeutic safety.',
    word_count = 330, read_time = '3 min read',
    metadata = coalesce(metadata, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"level":"Pharmacy Degree","group":"Degree","learning_objectives":["Distinguish pharmacodynamics and pharmacokinetics","Explain dose-response relationships","Describe therapeutic index"],"curriculum_aligned":true}'::jsonb,
    updated_at = now()
where slug = 'advanced-pharmacology-introduction';

update public.notes
set content = '<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Define a drug receptor and ligand.</li><li>Compare agonists, partial agonists and antagonists.</li><li>Distinguish affinity from efficacy.</li></ul><h2>Receptors and ligands</h2><p>A receptor is a biological macromolecule that recognises particular ligands and changes cellular activity when the appropriate ligand binds. A ligand may be an endogenous messenger, such as a hormone or neurotransmitter, or a medicine. Receptor binding is selective rather than perfectly exclusive: related molecules can sometimes bind the same target with different strengths and effects.</p><h2>Agonists and antagonists</h2><p>An agonist binds to a receptor and activates it to produce a response. A full agonist can produce the system&#39;s maximum response under the conditions studied. A partial agonist activates the receptor but produces a lower maximum response, even when it occupies many available receptors. An antagonist has affinity for the receptor but no activating efficacy of its own in that system; it reduces agonist action by preventing binding or activation. The effect of an antagonist depends on its mechanism and the concentrations of the interacting ligands.</p><h2>Affinity and efficacy</h2><p>Affinity describes how readily a ligand binds to a receptor. Efficacy describes its ability, after binding, to initiate a response. High affinity does not necessarily mean high efficacy: a molecule may bind strongly but activate the receptor weakly or not at all. Potency is also different; it relates to the concentration or dose required for a specified effect.</p><h2>Reversible competition</h2><p>A reversible competitive antagonist and an agonist compete for the same binding site. Increasing agonist concentration can often overcome this antagonism in a simplified experimental system, shifting the agonist concentration-response curve to the right without reducing its maximum response. Non-competitive or irreversible antagonism may reduce the maximum response, depending on receptor reserve and experimental conditions.</p><h2>Worked interpretation</h2><p>If compound A binds strongly but produces no receptor activation, it may be an antagonist rather than an agonist. If compound B activates the receptor but reaches a lower maximum response than a full agonist, it is consistent with partial agonism. These labels must be supported by experimental evidence.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Affinity concerns binding; efficacy concerns activation.</li><li>Potency concerns the amount needed for a defined response.</li><li>Receptor effects depend on the ligand, target, concentration and biological system.</li></ul>',
    content_preview = 'Understand receptor binding, agonists, partial agonists, antagonists, affinity, efficacy and concentration-response curves.',
    word_count = 350, read_time = '3 min read',
    metadata = coalesce(metadata, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"level":"Pharmacy Degree","group":"Degree","learning_objectives":["Define receptor and ligand","Compare agonists and antagonists","Distinguish affinity, efficacy and potency"],"curriculum_aligned":true}'::jsonb,
    updated_at = now()
where slug = 'drug-receptor-interactions-basics';

update public.notes
set content = '<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Identify major functional groups found in medicinal molecules.</li><li>Explain how structure can influence drug properties.</li><li>Describe why structure-activity relationships matter.</li></ul><h2>What is a functional group?</h2><p>A functional group is a recognisable arrangement of atoms within a molecule that contributes characteristic chemical behaviour. In medicinal chemistry, functional groups help determine ionisation, hydrogen bonding, solubility, stability, membrane permeability, metabolism and binding to a biological target. Their effects depend on the whole molecular structure and the environment; a functional group should not be interpreted in isolation.</p><h2>Common examples</h2><ul><li><strong>Alcohols and phenols:</strong> contain an –OH group. They can donate hydrogen bonds; phenols are generally more acidic than ordinary alcohols because their conjugate base is stabilised by the aromatic ring.</li><li><strong>Amines:</strong> can accept a proton. Many amines are ionised at physiological pH, which can improve aqueous solubility but may influence membrane passage.</li><li><strong>Amides:</strong> contain a carbonyl bonded to nitrogen. Their resonance affects basicity and geometry, and amide bonds occur in many biologically important molecules.</li><li><strong>Esters:</strong> may be hydrolysed by enzymes or chemical conditions. Ester formation is sometimes used to modify a compound&#39;s properties, including in prodrug design.</li><li><strong>Carboxylic acids:</strong> can lose a proton and often form negatively charged species at physiological pH.</li><li><strong>Ethers:</strong> contain an oxygen between carbon groups and can act as hydrogen-bond acceptors, but not donors.</li></ul><h2>Structure-activity relationships</h2><p>A structure-activity relationship (SAR) examines how deliberate changes in molecular structure alter biological activity. Replacing a group, changing its position or modifying molecular size may alter target binding, selectivity, metabolism or toxicity. Medicinal chemists use SAR evidence to guide optimisation, but a change that improves one property may worsen another.</p><h2>Solubility and ionisation</h2><p>Ionisable groups influence the balance between charged and uncharged forms. Charged forms often interact well with water, while the uncharged fraction may cross lipid membranes more readily. The actual behaviour depends on pH, pKa, formulation and molecular structure. These principles help explain why the same compound may behave differently in different environments.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Functional groups influence chemical behaviour and biological interactions.</li><li>Observed properties arise from the complete molecule and its environment.</li><li>SAR is evidence-led; structural changes can have both benefits and trade-offs.</li></ul>',
    content_preview = 'A practical guide to medicinal chemistry functional groups, ionisation, solubility and structure-activity relationships.',
    word_count = 390, read_time = '3 min read',
    metadata = coalesce(metadata, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"level":"Pharmacy Degree","group":"Degree","learning_objectives":["Recognise common functional groups","Relate structure to solubility and ionisation","Explain structure-activity relationships"],"curriculum_aligned":true}'::jsonb,
    updated_at = now()
where slug = 'medicinal-chemistry-functional-groups';

update public.notes
set content = '<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Distinguish descriptive, observational and experimental research.</li><li>Match a research question to an appropriate design.</li><li>Recognise bias, confounding and ethical requirements.</li></ul><h2>Start with the research question</h2><p>A research design is the plan used to answer a question in a transparent and reproducible way. The design should fit the population, exposure or intervention, outcome, time frame and available resources. A clear question helps researchers choose what data to collect and how to analyse it.</p><h2>Common study designs</h2><ul><li><strong>Descriptive studies</strong> summarise the frequency or distribution of a health event. They can identify patterns but generally cannot establish why those patterns occur.</li><li><strong>Cross-sectional studies</strong> measure characteristics at a particular time. They are useful for estimating prevalence, but exposure and outcome may be measured together, making temporal order difficult to establish.</li><li><strong>Cohort studies</strong> follow groups defined by exposure or another characteristic to compare subsequent outcomes. They can help establish sequence but may require long follow-up and can be affected by loss to follow-up.</li><li><strong>Case-control studies</strong> begin with people who have an outcome and compare their prior exposures with suitable controls. They can be efficient for rare outcomes but are vulnerable to selection and recall bias.</li><li><strong>Randomised controlled trials</strong> allocate eligible participants to interventions using randomisation. When properly conducted, randomisation helps balance known and unknown confounders, though it does not remove every source of bias.</li></ul><h2>Bias and confounding</h2><p>Bias is a systematic error in the design, conduct or analysis of a study. Confounding occurs when another factor is associated with both the exposure and outcome and distorts their relationship. Researchers can reduce these problems through careful sampling, measurement, randomisation where appropriate, restriction, matching and prespecified analytical methods.</p><h2>Ethics and data quality</h2><p>Research involving people requires appropriate ethical review, informed consent where applicable, privacy safeguards and a favourable balance of potential benefit and risk. Reliable results also depend on validated measurements, a suitable sample size, clear inclusion criteria and transparent reporting.</p><h2>Worked choice</h2><p>To estimate the proportion of adults currently using a medicine, a cross-sectional survey may be suitable. To investigate whether a previous exposure is associated with a rare adverse outcome, a case-control design may be efficient. To estimate the effect of an intervention, a well-designed randomised trial may be appropriate when ethical and feasible.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Choose a design based on the question, not convenience alone.</li><li>Association does not automatically establish causation.</li><li>Ethical conduct and transparent methods are essential to trustworthy evidence.</li></ul>',
    content_preview = 'Compare common research designs and learn how to choose a design while accounting for bias, confounding and ethics.',
    word_count = 420, read_time = '3 min read',
    metadata = coalesce(metadata, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"level":"Pharmacy Degree","group":"Degree","learning_objectives":["Compare research designs","Recognise bias and confounding","Apply core research ethics"],"curriculum_aligned":true}'::jsonb,
    updated_at = now()
where slug = 'research-methodology-study-designs';

update public.notes
set content = '<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Explain the main principles of antihypertensive treatment.</li><li>Recognise major medicine classes and their broad mechanisms.</li><li>Identify monitoring and safety considerations.</li></ul><h2>Why blood pressure is treated</h2><p>Persistent hypertension increases the risk of stroke, coronary heart disease, heart failure and kidney disease. Treatment aims to reduce long-term cardiovascular risk while considering the person&#39;s overall health, age, coexisting conditions, preferences and ability to follow the plan. Lifestyle measures and medicines may be used together.</p><h2>Major medicine classes</h2><ul><li><strong>ACE inhibitors</strong> reduce formation of angiotensin II and can lower vasoconstriction and aldosterone-mediated sodium retention.</li><li><strong>Angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs)</strong> block angiotensin II at the AT1 receptor.</li><li><strong>Calcium-channel blockers</strong> reduce calcium entry into vascular smooth muscle; some agents also affect cardiac rate and conduction.</li><li><strong>Thiazide-like diuretics</strong> increase renal sodium excretion and can reduce blood pressure through changes in fluid balance and vascular resistance.</li><li><strong>Beta blockers</strong> reduce beta-adrenergic effects on the heart and renin release. Their place in treatment depends on indications and patient factors.</li></ul><h2>Selection is individual</h2><p>There is no single medicine that is best for every patient. Choice depends on the blood-pressure level, cardiovascular and renal history, diabetes, pregnancy status, age, adverse-effect risk, interactions, local guidelines and availability. Combination therapy may be required when one medicine does not achieve the agreed target.</p><h2>Monitoring and precautions</h2><p>Monitoring may include blood pressure, adherence, symptoms and relevant laboratory tests. ACE inhibitors and ARBs can affect potassium and renal function and are generally avoided in pregnancy. Diuretics may alter electrolytes; calcium-channel blockers can cause ankle swelling; beta blockers may affect pulse and can mask some symptoms of hypoglycaemia. These are class-level considerations, not a substitute for checking the individual product information and current local guidance.</p><h2>Patient counselling</h2><p>Explain the purpose of treatment, how and when to take the prescribed medicine, common adverse effects, what monitoring is planned and when to seek professional advice. Patients should not stop or change treatment without discussing it with their healthcare professional. Urgent or severe symptoms require prompt assessment.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Hypertension management reduces future cardiovascular risk.</li><li>Medicine choice and monitoring are patient-specific.</li><li>Use current clinical guidelines and local prescribing information for real decisions.</li></ul><p><em>Educational note only; not an individual treatment recommendation.</em></p>',
    content_preview = 'An educational overview of antihypertensive medicine classes, mechanisms, monitoring and patient counselling.',
    word_count = 420, read_time = '3 min read',
    metadata = coalesce(metadata, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"level":"Pharmacy Degree","group":"Degree","learning_objectives":["Describe major antihypertensive classes","Identify monitoring needs","Explain patient counselling principles"],"curriculum_aligned":true,"clinical_note":true}'::jsonb,
    updated_at = now()
where slug = 'antihypertensive-drugs-overview';

-- Seven new notes: one for each curriculum group not yet represented by usable notes.
insert into public.notes
  (unit_id, slug, title, content, content_preview, author, category, tag, section_type,
   read_time, word_count, display_order, is_premium, is_active, metadata, toc)
values
(
 '97f7fd08-1714-42e6-a37f-eddd34a0008d',
 'photosynthesis-light-dependent-and-independent-reactions',
 'Photosynthesis: From Light to Stored Chemical Energy',
 '<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>State the overall purpose of photosynthesis.</li><li>Describe the roles of chlorophyll, light, water and carbon dioxide.</li><li>Explain how light intensity, carbon dioxide and temperature affect the rate.</li></ul><h2>What photosynthesis achieves</h2><p>Photosynthesis is the process by which green plants and some other organisms use light energy to synthesise organic molecules from carbon dioxide and water. In plants, the process takes place in chloroplasts. The captured energy becomes stored as chemical energy in carbohydrates, which can be used in respiration or converted into other substances needed for growth.</p><p>A simplified summary is: carbon dioxide + water, using light energy, produce carbohydrate and oxygen. The balanced equation often used for teaching is 6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂, with light energy and chlorophyll involved. This is an overall summary; the actual process occurs through many enzyme-controlled steps.</p><h2>Chloroplast structure</h2><p>Chlorophyll and other photosynthetic pigments are located in thylakoid membranes, which are arranged in stacks called grana. The surrounding fluid is the stroma. These compartments support different stages of photosynthesis.</p><h2>Light-dependent reactions</h2><p>Pigments absorb light and excite electrons in photosystems. Water is split to replace electrons, releasing oxygen, protons and electrons. Electron transport helps establish a proton gradient that drives ATP synthesis. Electrons also help reduce NADP⁺ to NADPH. ATP and NADPH provide energy and reducing power for carbon fixation. Oxygen released during photosynthesis comes from water, not from carbon dioxide.</p><h2>Carbon fixation</h2><p>In the Calvin cycle, carbon dioxide is incorporated into organic intermediates. Using ATP and NADPH from the light-dependent reactions, the cycle produces triose phosphate, some of which can be used to build carbohydrates while the rest helps regenerate the carbon acceptor. The cycle depends indirectly on light because its energy supply comes from the light-dependent reactions.</p><h2>Factors affecting rate</h2><p>Increasing light intensity can raise the rate when light is limiting, but eventually another factor becomes limiting. More carbon dioxide can increase the rate within a suitable range. Enzyme-controlled reactions are temperature-sensitive: a rise may increase rate to an optimum, while excessive heat disrupts enzymes and membranes. Water shortage can cause stomata to close, reducing carbon dioxide entry.</p><h2>Practical investigation</h2><p>When investigating light intensity with pondweed, keep temperature, carbon dioxide availability, plant length and acclimatisation time as constant as possible. Measure oxygen production over a defined interval, repeat measurements and calculate a mean. Moving a lamp changes both light intensity and temperature, so temperature should be controlled or monitored.</p><h2>Common misconception</h2><p>Plants respire as well as photosynthesise. Photosynthesis stores energy in organic molecules; cellular respiration releases usable energy from them. Oxygen production in light does not mean respiration stops.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Light-dependent reactions produce ATP and NADPH and release oxygen from water.</li><li>The Calvin cycle fixes carbon dioxide into organic molecules.</li><li>The rate is limited by whichever required factor is least available under the conditions.</li></ul>',
 'Photosynthesis explained through chloroplast structure, light-dependent reactions, carbon fixation and limiting factors.',
 'AliverBiopharm', 'Plant Nutrition', 'photosynthesis', 'lesson', '5 min read', 610, 1, false, true,
 '{"level":"O Level","group":"Form 2","curriculum_aligned":true,"learning_objectives":["Explain photosynthesis","Describe limiting factors","Plan a fair practical investigation"],"key_terms":["chloroplast","chlorophyll","thylakoid","carbon fixation","limiting factor"]}'::jsonb,
 '[]'::jsonb
),
(
 '059aa074-7228-458d-a6d2-c270bdb972f2',
 'homeostasis-negative-feedback',
 'Homeostasis: Keeping the Internal Environment Stable',
 '<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Define homeostasis.</li><li>Describe the parts of a negative-feedback system.</li><li>Explain how body temperature and blood glucose are regulated.</li></ul><h2>Meaning and importance</h2><p>Homeostasis is the regulation of internal conditions within limits that allow cells and enzymes to function effectively. Internal conditions do not remain perfectly constant; they fluctuate around a suitable range. Regulation is important because enzyme activity, membrane transport and cellular reactions are sensitive to factors such as temperature, water balance and glucose concentration.</p><h2>Negative feedback</h2><p>A typical control system includes a receptor that detects a change, a coordinating centre that compares information with a desired range, and an effector that produces a response. In negative feedback, the response opposes the original change and helps return the condition towards its normal range. The response usually reduces as the condition returns towards the range, preventing unnecessary correction.</p><h2>Temperature regulation</h2><p>When body temperature rises, thermoreceptors send information to the hypothalamus. Responses include increased sweating and widening of skin arterioles, which promote heat loss. When temperature falls, sweating decreases, skin arterioles narrow and skeletal muscles may shiver to generate heat. Behavioural responses, such as seeking shade or adding clothing, also contribute. These responses help regulate temperature but cannot always compensate for extreme environmental conditions.</p><h2>Blood glucose regulation</h2><p>After a meal, blood glucose rises and pancreatic beta cells release insulin. Insulin promotes glucose uptake in responsive tissues and encourages storage as glycogen, helping lower blood glucose. When blood glucose falls, pancreatic alpha cells release glucagon. Glucagon promotes processes in the liver that release glucose into the blood. Insulin and glucagon therefore have opposing effects in the overall regulation of blood glucose.</p><h2>Water balance</h2><p>Water balance is regulated partly through antidiuretic hormone (ADH). When the body needs to conserve water, increased ADH action makes collecting ducts in the kidneys more permeable to water, allowing more water to be reabsorbed and producing a smaller volume of more concentrated urine. When water is plentiful, ADH secretion falls and urine becomes more dilute.</p><h2>Applying feedback</h2><p>For a negative-feedback question, identify the variable, the direction of change, the receptor or sensor, the coordinating centre, the effector and how the response opposes the change. Do not confuse negative feedback with a harmful or negative outcome; the term describes the direction of the control response.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Homeostasis maintains conditions within a functional range.</li><li>Negative feedback counteracts deviations from that range.</li><li>Temperature, glucose and water balance use different sensors and effectors.</li></ul>',
 'Learn negative feedback and the regulation of temperature, blood glucose and water balance.',
 'AliverBiopharm', 'Homeostasis', 'negative-feedback', 'lesson', '4 min read', 520, 1, false, true,
 '{"level":"O Level","group":"Form 3","curriculum_aligned":true,"learning_objectives":["Define homeostasis","Explain negative feedback","Describe temperature and glucose regulation"],"key_terms":["homeostasis","negative feedback","hypothalamus","insulin","glucagon","ADH"]}'::jsonb,
 '[]'::jsonb
),
(
 '0ed34551-ca95-45c0-93a8-90eb7b93e23e',
 'genetics-genes-alleles-and-inheritance',
 'Genetics: Genes, Alleles and Inheritance',
 '<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Distinguish DNA, genes, alleles, genotype and phenotype.</li><li>Use a genetic cross to predict possible offspring genotypes.</li><li>Explain the limits of simple dominant-recessive models.</li></ul><h2>Basic terms</h2><p>DNA stores hereditary information. A gene is a DNA sequence that contributes to a functional product or helps regulate a biological process. Different versions of a gene are called alleles. An organism’s genotype describes its genetic make-up at the locus or loci being considered, while phenotype refers to observable characteristics produced through interactions between genotype and environment.</p><p>In a diploid organism, an individual usually carries two alleles for an autosomal gene, one inherited from each parent. A homozygous genotype contains two copies of the same allele; a heterozygous genotype contains two different alleles. In a simple complete-dominance model, the dominant allele determines the phenotype in a heterozygote, while the recessive phenotype appears when both alleles are recessive.</p><h2>Using a Punnett square</h2><p>Consider a hypothetical gene where T represents a dominant allele and t a recessive allele. A cross between two heterozygous individuals (Tt × Tt) produces gametes T and t from each parent. The possible combinations are TT, Tt, Tt and tt. Under complete dominance and assuming equal probability of fertilisation, the expected genotype ratio is 1 TT : 2 Tt : 1 tt, and the phenotype ratio is 3 dominant : 1 recessive.</p><p>These are probability expectations for many offspring, not a promise that every family will show exactly those proportions. Each fertilisation is an independent event.</p><h2>Meiosis and variation</h2><p>During meiosis, homologous chromosomes separate so gametes receive one chromosome from each pair. Crossing over and independent assortment create new allele combinations. Random fertilisation adds further variation. Mutations can introduce new alleles, while recombination reshuffles existing variation.</p><h2>Beyond simple dominance</h2><p>Not all inheritance follows a single dominant-recessive pattern. In incomplete dominance, a heterozygote has an intermediate phenotype. In codominance, both alleles contribute recognisably to the phenotype. Some traits are controlled by many genes and influenced by environmental factors. Sex-linked inheritance depends on genes located on sex chromosomes and requires attention to the chromosome combinations involved.</p><h2>Exam approach</h2><p>Write down the parental genotypes, identify the gametes each parent can produce, complete the cross, and state both genotype and phenotype probabilities. Define any symbols before using them and state the assumptions, especially whether dominance is complete and whether the trait is autosomal.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Alleles are alternative versions of a gene.</li><li>Genotype and phenotype are related but not identical.</li><li>Punnett squares show probabilities, not guaranteed family outcomes.</li><li>Real traits may involve multiple genes and environmental effects.</li></ul>',
 'Build confidence with genes, alleles, genotype, phenotype, Punnett squares, meiosis and inheritance patterns.',
 'AliverBiopharm', 'Genetics', 'inheritance', 'lesson', '4 min read', 540, 1, false, true,
 '{"level":"O Level","group":"Form 4","curriculum_aligned":true,"learning_objectives":["Define genetic terminology","Complete a monohybrid cross","Compare inheritance patterns"],"key_terms":["gene","allele","genotype","phenotype","homozygous","heterozygous","meiosis"]}'::jsonb,
 '[]'::jsonb
),
(
 '67245ca2-e8b7-4992-9fe5-3cb75b09a604',
 'enzyme-action-factors-and-inhibition',
 'Enzymes: Mechanism, Specificity and Factors Affecting Activity',
 '<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Explain how enzymes catalyse reactions.</li><li>Relate enzyme activity to temperature, pH and substrate concentration.</li><li>Distinguish competitive and non-competitive inhibition.</li></ul><h2>Enzymes as biological catalysts</h2><p>Enzymes are biological catalysts that increase reaction rates by lowering activation energy. They are not consumed by the reaction and do not change the overall energy difference between reactants and products or the equilibrium position. Most enzymes are proteins, although some RNA molecules also have catalytic activity.</p><h2>Active site and specificity</h2><p>A substrate binds at an enzyme’s active site, forming an enzyme-substrate complex. Interactions at the active site help stabilise the transition state and facilitate conversion to products. The active site is not always a rigid lock; its shape and chemical environment can adjust as the substrate binds, a concept often described as induced fit. Specificity arises from complementary shape, charge, polarity and other interactions.</p><h2>Temperature and pH</h2><p>Raising temperature usually increases molecular movement and collision frequency up to an optimum. Above the optimum, interactions maintaining the enzyme’s structure may be disrupted, changing the active site and reducing activity. Each enzyme has a pH range in which it functions best. Extreme pH can alter ionisation and structure, reducing catalysis. Optima depend on the enzyme and its normal environment.</p><h2>Substrate concentration</h2><p>At low substrate concentration, increasing substrate usually increases reaction rate because more enzyme active sites encounter substrate. At high concentration, most active sites may be occupied and the rate approaches a maximum, Vmax. The Michaelis constant, Km, is the substrate concentration at half Vmax in the Michaelis-Menten model; its interpretation as a direct measure of binding affinity requires assumptions and should not be applied universally.</p><h2>Inhibition</h2><p>A competitive inhibitor competes with substrate for the active site. In a simple reversible model, increasing substrate can reduce the inhibitor’s effect, and apparent Km increases while Vmax remains unchanged. A pure non-competitive inhibitor binds at a different site and reduces catalytic capacity; in the simple model Vmax decreases while Km is unchanged. Real inhibitors can show mixed behaviour, so kinetic evidence is needed to classify them accurately.</p><h2>Practical investigation</h2><p>When testing temperature or pH, vary one independent variable at a time, keep enzyme and substrate concentrations constant, measure product formation or substrate disappearance over a fixed interval, repeat trials and use a suitable control. Report the method and limitations so the results can be reproduced.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Enzymes lower activation energy and are not used up.</li><li>Activity depends on conditions and can fall when structure is disrupted.</li><li>Inhibitor effects are best interpreted from mechanism and experimental evidence.</li></ul>',
 'Explore enzyme catalysis, active-site specificity, temperature, pH, substrate concentration and inhibition.',
 'AliverBiopharm', 'Enzymes', 'enzyme-action', 'lesson', '5 min read', 610, 1, false, true,
 '{"level":"A Level","group":"Form 5","curriculum_aligned":true,"learning_objectives":["Explain enzyme catalysis","Interpret effects of temperature and pH","Compare inhibition models"],"key_terms":["active site","activation energy","Vmax","Km","competitive inhibition","non-competitive inhibition"]}'::jsonb,
 '[]'::jsonb
),
(
 '1a6da14f-a371-41cd-800b-9885c4769d6a',
 'advanced-genetics-linkage-crossing-over-and-mapping',
 'Advanced Genetics: Linkage, Crossing Over and Gene Mapping',
 '<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Explain linkage and recombination.</li><li>Interpret parental and recombinant offspring classes.</li><li>Estimate genetic map distance from suitable test-cross data.</li></ul><h2>Linked genes</h2><p>Genes located on the same chromosome are physically linked. They do not assort independently as often as genes on different chromosomes, although crossing over during prophase I of meiosis can separate alleles on the same chromosome. The closer two loci are, the less likely a crossover will occur between them in a single meiosis.</p><h2>Crossing over and recombination</h2><p>During prophase I, homologous chromosomes pair and non-sister chromatids can exchange corresponding DNA segments. This recombination creates new combinations of alleles. In a test cross, offspring classes resembling the original parental combinations are called parental types; those with new combinations are recombinant types. A high proportion of parental types suggests linkage, provided the cross and scoring are appropriate.</p><h2>Estimating map distance</h2><p>For a suitable test cross, recombination frequency is calculated as recombinant offspring divided by total offspring, multiplied by 100. One percent recombination is conventionally equivalent to one map unit or centimorgan (cM) for short distances. For example, if 180 of 1,000 offspring are recombinant, the estimated recombination frequency is 18%, corresponding to about 18 cM under the simple mapping convention.</p><p>Recombination frequency cannot exceed 50%. For genes far apart on the same chromosome, multiple crossovers may restore parental combinations and cause the observed frequency to underestimate the physical distance. Map distance is therefore an estimate, not a direct measurement of DNA length.</p><h2>Three-point crosses</h2><p>In a three-point test cross, the most frequent offspring classes are usually parental, while the least frequent classes often reflect double crossovers. Comparing parental and double-crossover classes can help identify the middle gene. This analysis requires careful classification of offspring and sufficient sample size; rare classes may be affected by chance or scoring errors.</p><h2>Sources of variation and limitations</h2><p>Recombination rates can differ by chromosome region, sex and organism. Small samples produce uncertain estimates, and viability differences may change observed offspring ratios. Linkage analysis should therefore state assumptions and report actual counts as well as calculated percentages.</p><h2>Worked calculation</h2><p>Suppose a test cross yields 72 recombinant offspring among 400 total offspring. Recombination frequency = (72 ÷ 400) × 100 = 18%. The estimated map distance is 18 cM for this pair of loci under the simple convention.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Linkage reflects genes on the same chromosome.</li><li>Crossing over produces recombinant allele combinations.</li><li>Observed recombination frequency is an estimate with biological and sampling limits.</li></ul>',
 'Study linkage, crossing over, recombination frequency and genetic map-distance calculations.',
 'AliverBiopharm', 'Advanced Genetics', 'gene-linkage', 'lesson', '5 min read', 560, 1, false, true,
 '{"level":"A Level","group":"Form 6","curriculum_aligned":true,"learning_objectives":["Explain linkage and crossing over","Calculate recombination frequency","Interpret test-cross data"],"key_terms":["linkage","recombinant","parental type","centimorgan","test cross"]}'::jsonb,
 '[]'::jsonb
),
(
 '62f176ee-0381-4da2-849d-d8de510a6eb9',
 'pharmacodynamics-dose-response-and-receptors',
 'Pharmacodynamics: Receptors, Dose and Response',
 '<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Define pharmacodynamics and receptor-mediated action.</li><li>Distinguish potency from efficacy.</li><li>Explain why response and dose are not interchangeable.</li></ul><h2>What pharmacodynamics means</h2><p>Pharmacodynamics studies the biochemical and physiological effects of medicines and the mechanisms that produce them. Many medicines act at receptors, enzymes, ion channels or transporters. Binding to a target can activate a pathway, inhibit a process or alter the movement of ions or molecules across membranes.</p><h2>Receptor agonists and antagonists</h2><p>An agonist binds to a receptor and activates it. A partial agonist produces a lower maximum response than a full agonist in the same system. An antagonist binds without activating the receptor and reduces the effect of an agonist. The clinical effect depends on the target, the medicine concentration, the amount of target available and the physiology of the patient.</p><h2>Potency and efficacy</h2><p>Potency describes how much medicine is needed to produce a defined effect. Efficacy describes the maximum effect that can be produced under stated conditions. A medicine can be more potent without having a higher maximum efficacy. Comparing potency alone is not enough to decide which medicine is clinically preferable; safety, evidence, duration, formulation and patient needs also matter.</p><h2>Dose-response relationships</h2><p>A graded dose-response curve shows how response changes as dose or concentration increases. The response often rises over a range and then approaches a maximum when the relevant system becomes saturated or limited. ED50 may refer to the dose producing 50% of a defined effect in a population or experimental setting; the exact meaning should be stated. It is not automatically the dose appropriate for an individual patient.</p><h2>Therapeutic window and safety</h2><p>The therapeutic window is the range of exposure associated with useful effects without unacceptable toxicity. It varies by medicine and patient. Adverse reactions, interactions, organ function, age, pregnancy, adherence and genetics may alter the balance between benefit and harm. A medicine&#39;s dose should follow an authorised prescription and current clinical guidance, not a classroom calculation alone.</p><h2>Applied example</h2><p>If medicine A produces a specified response at a lower concentration than medicine B, A is more potent for that endpoint in the experiment. If both reach the same maximum response, their measured efficacy may be similar despite different potency. This comparison does not establish which is safer.</p><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Pharmacodynamics links target interaction to physiological response.</li><li>Potency and efficacy describe different features of a dose-response curve.</li><li>Individual treatment decisions require clinical assessment and authoritative guidance.</li></ul><p><em>Educational material only; it does not replace professional prescribing or patient-specific advice.</em></p>',
 'Learn receptor action, dose-response curves, potency, efficacy and therapeutic-window concepts.',
 'AliverBiopharm', 'Basic Pharmacology', 'pharmacodynamics', 'lesson', '4 min read', 510, 1, false, true,
 '{"level":"Pharmacy","group":"Certificate","curriculum_aligned":true,"learning_objectives":["Define pharmacodynamics","Compare potency and efficacy","Explain therapeutic window"],"key_terms":["agonist","antagonist","potency","efficacy","dose-response","therapeutic window"],"clinical_note":true}'::jsonb,
 '[]'::jsonb
),
(
 '102a7a2e-5d91-4ca9-8464-ae54a478ab70',
 'pharmaceutical-calculations-units-concentrations-and-doses',
 'Pharmaceutical Calculations: Units, Concentrations and Doses',
 '<h2>Learning objectives</h2><ul><li>Convert common metric units accurately.</li><li>Calculate concentration and a required volume from a stated dose.</li><li>Use dimensional analysis to check the units in an answer.</li></ul><h2>Why units matter</h2><p>Pharmaceutical calculations require consistent units and careful interpretation of the prescribed or supplied information. A numerically plausible answer can still be unsafe if the units are wrong. Always write the units beside each value, convert before calculating, and check whether the final quantity makes sense for the formulation and context.</p><h2>Useful metric relationships</h2><ul><li>1 g = 1,000 mg.</li><li>1 mg = 1,000 micrograms (µg).</li><li>1 L = 1,000 mL.</li><li>1% w/v means 1 g of substance in 100 mL of final preparation, equivalent to 10 mg/mL when the percentage is defined on a mass/volume basis.</li></ul><p>Write micrograms clearly as µg and avoid ambiguous abbreviations. Do not assume that all percentage strengths are w/v; the basis may be w/w or v/v and must be checked.</p><h2>Concentration</h2><p>For a mass-per-volume concentration, concentration = mass ÷ volume. If a solution contains 250 mg in 5 mL, the concentration is 250 mg ÷ 5 mL = 50 mg/mL. The units show how much mass is present in each millilitre.</p><h2>Calculating a volume</h2><p>When the required dose and concentration use compatible units, volume required = prescribed dose ÷ concentration. For a purely educational example, if a verified order requires 100 mg and the supplied concentration is 50 mg/mL, the arithmetic gives 100 mg ÷ 50 mg/mL = 2 mL. The milligram units cancel, leaving millilitres. This example is not a recommendation to administer a medicine; the order, product, patient, route and clinical appropriateness must be independently verified.</p><h2>Dimensional analysis</h2><p>Keep units in every step. Convert grams to milligrams before dividing if the concentration is expressed in mg/mL. If the units do not cancel to the unit requested, stop and correct the setup rather than guessing. Avoid rounding intermediate values unnecessarily; round the final result only according to the applicable protocol and measuring device.</p><h2>Safety checks</h2><ul><li>Confirm the medicine, formulation, strength, route and intended dose.</li><li>Check whether the value is per dose, per day, per kilogram or per administration.</li><li>Use an independent check for high-risk medicines or calculations required by local policy.</li><li>Ask a qualified supervisor when the order or units are unclear.</li></ul><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul><li>Convert units before calculating.</li><li>Use concentration and dimensional analysis, not memorised numbers alone.</li><li>Mathematical correctness does not replace clinical verification.</li></ul><p><em>For learning only. Real dispensing or administration requires verification against the authorised order, product information and local procedures.</em></p>',
 'Practise metric conversions, mass-per-volume concentration and dose-volume calculations with unit checks.',
 'AliverBiopharm', 'Pharmaceutical Calculations', 'concentration-and-dose-calculation', 'lesson', '4 min read', 500, 1, false, true,
 '{"level":"Pharmacy","group":"Diploma","curriculum_aligned":true,"learning_objectives":["Convert metric units","Calculate concentration","Use dimensional analysis"],"key_terms":["concentration","mg/mL","metric conversion","dimensional analysis"],"clinical_note":true}'::jsonb,
 '[]'::jsonb
)
on conflict (slug) do update set
  unit_id = excluded.unit_id,
  title = excluded.title,
  content = excluded.content,
  content_preview = excluded.content_preview,
  author = excluded.author,
  category = excluded.category,
  tag = excluded.tag,
  section_type = excluded.section_type,
  read_time = excluded.read_time,
  word_count = excluded.word_count,
  display_order = excluded.display_order,
  is_premium = excluded.is_premium,
  is_active = excluded.is_active,
  metadata = coalesce(public.notes.metadata, '{}'::jsonb) || excluded.metadata,
  toc = '[]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now();

-- Refresh only generated legacy links for notes, preserving any manually curated relationships.
delete from public.learning_content_links
where content_type = 'note'
  and link_origin = 'legacy_unit_id';

insert into public.learning_content_links
  (curriculum_unit_id, content_type, content_id, relationship_type, is_primary, link_origin)
select unit_id, 'note', id::text, 'primary', true, 'legacy_unit_id'
from public.notes
where unit_id is not null
  and is_active = true
on conflict (curriculum_unit_id, content_type, content_id, relationship_type)
do update set is_primary = true, link_origin = 'legacy_unit_id', updated_at = now();

commit;
